      "math_academy_id": "e-21830",
      "problem": "Let $F(x)=x^3 - x + 5\\cos x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-\\pi,\\pi).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in(-\\pi,0)?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14227911/assets/0b4d33840ea40cea57ba69bf7c86e262ce44cd2db782ff0fee10b2890e2057de.svg)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
